{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SovMurZotYPnuGoLNfVXoe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n2\t\n3\tCompleted: Add React 19.2 cacheSignal() for Server Component fetch cleanup (ID: US-162)\n4\t\n5\t### What was implemented\n6\t- Created `lib/cache-utils.ts` with two functions:\n7\t  - `getCachedData()`: Generic utility that wraps fetch with cache() and cacheSignal()\n8\t  - `getCachedRoomAvailability()`: Example implementation for room availability API calls\n9\t- Updated `Rooms.tsx` Server Component to import and demonstrate cacheSignal() pattern\n10\t- Added try-catch error handling to gracefully handle AbortError when cache is invalidated\n11\t- No TypeScript errors; all 83 pages generated successfully during build\n12\t\n13\t### Patterns discovered\n14\t- React 19.2 cacheSignal() is only available in Server Components (not Client Components or Route Handlers)\n15\t- cacheSignal() returns an AbortSignal that fires when cache lifetime ends\n16\t- Pattern: `const dedupedFetch = cache((url) => fetch(url, { signal: cacheSignal() }))`\n17\t- AbortError has name property that can be checked to distinguish cache invalidation from network errors\n18\t- Graceful error handling prevents cache-related aborts from breaking the page\n19\t\n20\t### Gotchas\n21\t- cacheSignal() is a React 19.2 feature; requires Next.js 16+ for full support\n22\t- Must import both `cache` and `cacheSignal` from \"react\" (not from \"next/cache\")\n23\t- External API calls may fail during SSR (expected for example APIs) — always wrap in try-catch\n24\t\n25\t## Iteration US-161 (Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n26\t\n27\tCompleted: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161)\n28\t\n29\t### What was implemented\n30\t- Replaced `useCallback` with `useEffectEvent` in RoomsEcommerceTracker.tsx and BookEcommerceTracker.tsx\n31\t- Removed analytics click handlers from useEffect dependency arrays\n32\t- Effects now run once on mount, not on every callback identity change\n33\t\n34\t### Patterns discovered\n35\t- Code was already committed in attempt 2 (d9fa754) but diff guard failed due to unstaged files inflating diff count\n36\t- Attempt 3 only needed to verify code was in place and mark passes: true\n37\t- When prior attempts commit code but fail post-commit gates, verify existing commits before re-implementing\n38\t\n39\t### Gotchas\n40\t- SPIRAL diff guard counts ALL staged/unstaged changes, not just story-related files — always use `git add <specific-files>` not `git add -A`\n41\t- Previous attempts failed at 540 and 23068 lines because unrelated modified files were included in the diff measurement\n42\t\n43\t## Iteration US-144 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n44\t\n45\tCompleted: Replace JavaScript tooltip positioning with CSS Popover API and Anchor Positioning (ID: US-144)\n46\t\n47\t### What was implemented\n48\t- Created `InfoPopover.tsx` component using native HTML `popover` attribute, `popoverTarget`, CSS `anchor-name`/`position-anchor`\n49\t- Added popover + anchor positioning CSS in globals.css with `@supports` fallback for browsers without anchor positioning\n50\t- Converted Header.tsx search button `title` tooltip to InfoPopover\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"064c2877-3a27-4cc4-93b1-67ad4599ceeb","uuid":"6d02b381-fbb1-4543-b09c-c4c87170f4c3","timestamp":"2026-04-14T06:57:23.615Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\progress.txt","content":"## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Add React 19.2 cacheSignal() for Server Component fetch cleanup (ID: US-162)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `lib/cache-utils.ts` with two functions:\n  - `getCachedData()`: Generic utility that wraps fetch with cache() and cacheSignal()\n  - `getCachedRoomAvailability()`: Example implementation for room availability API calls\n- Updated `Rooms.tsx` Server Component to import and demonstrate cacheSignal() pattern\n- Added try-catch error handling to gracefully handle AbortError when cache is invalidated\n- No TypeScript errors; all 83 pages generated successfully during build\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- React 19.2 cacheSignal() is only available in Server Components (not Client Components or Route Handlers)\n- cacheSignal() returns an AbortSignal that fires when cache lifetime ends\n- Pattern: `const dedupedFetch = cache((url) => fetch(url, { signal: cacheSignal() }))`\n- AbortError has name property that can be checked to distinguish cache invalidation from network errors\n- Graceful error handling prevents cache-related aborts from breaking the page\n\n### Gotchas\n- cacheSignal() is a React 19.2 feature; requires Next.js 16+ for full support\n- Must import both `cache` and `cacheSignal` from \"react\" (not from \"next/cache\")\n- External API calls may fail during SSR (expected for example APIs) — always wrap in try-catch\n\n## Iteration US-161 (Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Replaced `useCallback` with `useEffectEvent` in RoomsEcommerceTracker.tsx and BookEcommerceTracker.tsx\n- Removed analytics click handlers from useEffect dependency arrays\n- Effects now run once on mount, not on every callback identity change\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Code was already committed in attempt 2 (d9fa754) but diff guard failed due to unstaged files inflating diff count\n- Attempt 3 only needed to verify code was in place and mark passes: true\n- When prior attempts commit code but fail post-commit gates, verify existing commits before re-implementing\n\n### Gotchas\n- SPIRAL diff guard counts ALL staged/unstaged changes, not just story-related files — always use `git add <specific-files>` not `git add -A`\n- Previous attempts failed at 540 and 23068 lines because unrelated modified files were included in the diff measurement\n\n## Iteration US-144 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Replace JavaScript tooltip positioning with CSS Popover API and Anchor Positioning (ID: US-144)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `InfoPopover.tsx` component using native HTML `popover` attribute, `popoverTarget`, CSS `anchor-name`/`position-anchor`\n- Added popover + anchor positioning CSS in globals.css with `@supports` fallback for browsers without anchor positioning\n- Converted Header.tsx search button `title` tooltip to InfoPopover","numLines":50,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1400}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01FkqLZLyNVwf3dMLbm1Nuv4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me add a new iteration entry at the beginning of progress.txt for US-173.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1110,"cache_read_input_tokens":88229,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1110},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"064c2877-3a27-4cc4-93b1-67ad4599ceeb","uuid":"611b1987-c909-4d31-ab4d-ffc7e388f4d6"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01FkqLZLyNVwf3dMLbm1Nuv4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EVE1EC58bJDPiGN9Wxrzm2","name":"Edit","input":{"replace_all":false,"file_path":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\progress.txt","old_string":"## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026","new_string":"## Iteration US-173 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Implement A/B testing with Vercel Feature Flags and Edge Config (ID: US-173)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `site/vercel.json` with Next.js framework configuration and environment variables:\n  - NEXT_PUBLIC_FLAG_CTA_VARIANT (default: \"book-now\") for local development fallback\n  - EDGE_CONFIG placeholder for production Vercel Edge Config connection\n- Verified complete A/B testing implementation already in place:\n  - `flags.ts`: Flag types (CTAVariant with 3 options) and getVariant() function\n  - `proxy.ts`: Server-side flag evaluation in Next.js 16 proxy (formerly middleware)\n  - `site/src/app/book/page.tsx`: Reads variant from response headers (zero layout shift)\n  - `BookEcommerceTracker.tsx`: GA4 custom dimension (dimension_1) records variant for statistical analysis\n- Updated `.env.local` with NEXT_PUBLIC_FLAG_CTA_VARIANT environment variable\n- Verified build passes: all 68+ static pages generated successfully\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Next.js 16 uses `proxy()` function instead of middleware.ts (both cannot coexist in same project)\n- `proxy.ts` already had A/B testing logic in place but vercel.json was missing\n- Previous attempt failed with 12854-line oversized diff due to unrelated file modifications\n- This iteration focused on minimal required configuration: vercel.json + env setup verification\n- All acceptance criteria were already implemented by prior work; this iteration completes the story by adding the missing vercel.json configuration\n\n### Gotchas\n- Vercel Flags UI in dashboard requires EDGE_CONFIG environment variable to be set in Vercel project settings\n- .env.local should NOT be committed (already in .gitignore); only .env.local.example and vercel.json are version-controlled\n- Next.js 16 proxy() is the evolution of middleware.ts; cannot have both files in src/ root\n- Flag values must be evaluated server-side in proxy() or middleware to prevent client-side flicker\n\n## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1110,"cache_read_input_tokens":88229,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1110},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"064c2877-3a27-4cc4-93b1-67ad4599ceeb","uuid":"f1b9f7d4-f39f-40ac-9ed2-1967ac9b739e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EVE1EC58bJDPiGN9Wxrzm2","type":"tool_result","content":"The file C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\progress.txt has been updated successfully."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"064c2877-3a27-4cc4-93b1-67ad4599ceeb","uuid":"cc75a16e-c7c2-4437-a532-948819869c80","timestamp":"2026-04-14T06:57:32.283Z","tool_use_result":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\Jyue\\Documents\\1-projects\\Software Projects\\pelangi-website\\progress.txt","oldString":"## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026","newString":"## Iteration US-173 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Implement A/B testing with Vercel Feature Flags and Edge Config (ID: US-173)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `site/vercel.json` with Next.js framework configuration and environment variables:\n  - NEXT_PUBLIC_FLAG_CTA_VARIANT (default: \"book-now\") for local development fallback\n  - EDGE_CONFIG placeholder for production Vercel Edge Config connection\n- Verified complete A/B testing implementation already in place:\n  - `flags.ts`: Flag types (CTAVariant with 3 options) and getVariant() function\n  - `proxy.ts`: Server-side flag evaluation in Next.js 16 proxy (formerly middleware)\n  - `site/src/app/book/page.tsx`: Reads variant from response headers (zero layout shift)\n  - `BookEcommerceTracker.tsx`: GA4 custom dimension (dimension_1) records variant for statistical analysis\n- Updated `.env.local` with NEXT_PUBLIC_FLAG_CTA_VARIANT environment variable\n- Verified build passes: all 68+ static pages generated successfully\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Next.js 16 uses `proxy()` function instead of middleware.ts (both cannot coexist in same project)\n- `proxy.ts` already had A/B testing logic in place but vercel.json was missing\n- Previous attempt failed with 12854-line oversized diff due to unrelated file modifications\n- This iteration focused on minimal required configuration: vercel.json + env setup verification\n- All acceptance criteria were already implemented by prior work; this iteration completes the story by adding the missing vercel.json configuration\n\n### Gotchas\n- Vercel Flags UI in dashboard requires EDGE_CONFIG environment variable to be set in Vercel project settings\n- .env.local should NOT be committed (already in .gitignore); only .env.local.example and vercel.json are version-controlled\n- Next.js 16 proxy() is the evolution of middleware.ts; cannot have both files in src/ root\n- Flag values must be evaluated server-side in proxy() or middleware to prevent client-side flicker\n\n## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026","originalFile":"## Iteration US-162 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Add React 19.2 cacheSignal() for Server Component fetch cleanup (ID: US-162)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `lib/cache-utils.ts` with two functions:\n  - `getCachedData()`: Generic utility that wraps fetch with cache() and cacheSignal()\n  - `getCachedRoomAvailability()`: Example implementation for room availability API calls\n- Updated `Rooms.tsx` Server Component to import and demonstrate cacheSignal() pattern\n- Added try-catch error handling to gracefully handle AbortError when cache is invalidated\n- No TypeScript errors; all 83 pages generated successfully during build\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- React 19.2 cacheSignal() is only available in Server Components (not Client Components or Route Handlers)\n- cacheSignal() returns an AbortSignal that fires when cache lifetime ends\n- Pattern: `const dedupedFetch = cache((url) => fetch(url, { signal: cacheSignal() }))`\n- AbortError has name property that can be checked to distinguish cache invalidation from network errors\n- Graceful error handling prevents cache-related aborts from breaking the page\n\n### Gotchas\n- cacheSignal() is a React 19.2 feature; requires Next.js 16+ for full support\n- Must import both `cache` and `cacheSignal` from \"react\" (not from \"next/cache\")\n- External API calls may fail during SSR (expected for example APIs) — always wrap in try-catch\n\n## Iteration US-161 (Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Implement React 19.2 useEffectEvent for stable analytics event handlers (ID: US-161)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Replaced `useCallback` with `useEffectEvent` in RoomsEcommerceTracker.tsx and BookEcommerceTracker.tsx\n- Removed analytics click handlers from useEffect dependency arrays\n- Effects now run once on mount, not on every callback identity change\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Code was already committed in attempt 2 (d9fa754) but diff guard failed due to unstaged files inflating diff count\n- Attempt 3 only needed to verify code was in place and mark passes: true\n- When prior attempts commit code but fail post-commit gates, verify existing commits before re-implementing\n\n### Gotchas\n- SPIRAL diff guard counts ALL staged/unstaged changes, not just story-related files — always use `git add <specific-files>` not `git add -A`\n- Previous attempts failed at 540 and 23068 lines because unrelated modified files were included in the diff measurement\n\n## Iteration US-144 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Replace JavaScript tooltip positioning with CSS Popover API and Anchor Positioning (ID: US-144)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Created `InfoPopover.tsx` component using native HTML `popover` attribute, `popoverTarget`, CSS `anchor-name`/`position-anchor`\n- Added popover + anchor positioning CSS in globals.css with `@supports` fallback for browsers without anchor positioning\n- Converted Header.tsx search button `title` tooltip to InfoPopover\n- Converted HeroTrustBadges.tsx trust badge buttons to InfoPopover with contextual content\n- No JS tooltip library existed to remove (no Popper.js/Floating UI in the project)\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- React 19 includes `popover` and `popoverTarget` in its type definitions — no `@ts-expect-error` needed\n- `CSS.escape()` is a browser API not available in Node.js SSR — use regex sanitization instead for `useId()` output\n- Attempt 1 failed with 17,788 line diff — caused by touching package-lock.json or similar large files\n- Keep implementation under 500 lines total insertions+deletions\n\n### Gotchas\n- `useId()` returns colon-wrapped strings like `:r0:` — must sanitize for CSS custom property names\n- Linter may auto-revert changes if import is added separately from usage\n\n## Iteration US-143 (FINAL - Attempt 3) - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Apply CSS @starting-style for GPU-accelerated entry animations (ID: US-143)\n\n### What was implemented\n- Added @starting-style CSS utility classes (.entry-fade, .entry-zoom-in, .entry-fade-up) to globals.css\n- Classes use only opacity + transform for GPU compositor thread\n- Includes prefers-reduced-motion: reduce override that disables transitions\n- Components modified in commit b3c2354: CookieConsent, ExitIntentOverlay, RoomsTabsClient, SearchDialog\n- CSS classes added in commit 9d11b13\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Attempt 1 failed: oversized diff (14829 lines > 500 limit)\n- Attempt 2 failed: missing diagnosis block (process gate)\n- Attempt 3 succeeded: code was already committed, just needed verification and passes=true\n- When implementation is already committed, verify in-place rather than re-implementing\n\n### Gotchas\n- SPIRAL may revert prd.json passes flag between attempts even if code is committed\n- The TurnstileWidget TS error is pre-existing (missing @marsidev/react-turnstile dep) and unrelated\n- Always output diagnosis block BEFORE any file edits to pass SPIRAL process gate\n\n## Iteration US-249 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Blog post - 5 Gig Jobs for Malaysians Waiting for Singapore Work Approval (ID: US-249)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n  - Added new blog post object with slug `gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass`\n  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"9 min read\"\n  - 5 gig economy job options with earnings estimates:\n    - Foodpanda Delivery Rider: RM1,800–3,500/month\n    - Lalamove Driver: RM2,000–3,800/month\n    - Grab Ride/GrabFood Driver: RM2,500–4,200/month\n    - Part-time F&B/Cafe Work: RM1,500–2,500/month\n    - Online Freelancing: RM1,200–5,000+/month\n  - Each job includes: requirements, earnings breakdown, pros, and cons\n  - Sections on combining gigs, tax/legal notes, Pelangi guest relevance\n  - Internal links to `/rooms` (Pelangi Capsule)\n  - Intro references Pelangi guests in work pass waiting period\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Blog post HTML content needs careful optimization to stay under 8000 character limit\n- Initial draft (8191 chars) required condensing descriptions by ~35% to fit limit\n- Final optimized content: 5315 characters (well within limit)\n- Consistent format: metadata + HTML content with proper spacing and paragraph structure\n\n### Test results\n- Content size: 5315 characters (under 8000 limit) ✓\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (all static pages generated)\n- All internal links present: `/rooms` (2 times) ✓\n- All acceptance criteria met:\n  - ✓ Slug 'gig-jobs-malaysia-waiting-singapore-work-pass', category 'Career & Work'\n  - ✓ 5 gig job options with earnings estimates\n  - ✓ Intro references Pelangi guests\n  - ✓ readTime '9 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n  - ✓ npm run build passes\n\n## Iteration US-244 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Blog post - Living in JB Working in Singapore — Cross-Border Commuter Guide 2026 (ID: US-244)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n  - Added new blog post object with slug `jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026`\n  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"8 min read\"\n  - Comprehensive HTML table of 5 bus services with routes, prices, duration, and frequency:\n    - Causeway Link: RM5–6 / SGD2–2.50\n    - SBS Transit: RM3–4 / SGD1.50\n    - Sunrays Express: RM5 / SGD2\n    - Premium (Intercity): RM8–12 / SGD4–5\n    - Personal vehicle: RM30–50 (toll + petrol)\n  - Monthly cost breakdown table with 7 expense categories (rent, transport, food, utilities, phone, sundries)\n  - Shows monthly savings of SGD4,265–4,665 (SGD50,000–56,000 annually)\n  - Sections on CIQ process, peak hour tips, housing strategy, commute sustainability\n  - Internal links to `/rooms` (Pelangi Capsule) and `/getting-here` for directions\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Blog post HTML content remains under 8000 character limit (7,378 chars in this case)\n- Reading time is auto-computed from HTML word count via `computeReadingTime()` function\n- Blog slugs use kebab-case format with date suffix for blog posts\n- Tables provide structured comparison and cost data for complex topics\n\n### Test results\n- Content size: 7,378 characters (well under 8,000 limit)\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (68 static pages including new blog post)\n- All internal links present: `/rooms` (3 times), `/getting-here` (1 time)\n- All acceptance criteria met:\n  - ✓ Slug 'jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026', category 'Career & Work'\n  - ✓ HTML list of 5 bus services with prices and details\n  - ✓ Monthly cost breakdown with 7 expense categories\n  - ✓ Internal links to /getting-here and /rooms\n  - ✓ readTime '8 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n  - ✓ npm run build passes\n\n## Iteration US-243 - Mon, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Blog post - Singapore Work Pass Guide for Malaysians (ID: US-243)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Blog post** (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`):\n  - Added new blog post object with slug `singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians`\n  - Category: \"Career & Work\", date: \"2026-04-14\", readTime: \"12 min read\"\n  - Comprehensive HTML comparison table of 3 Singapore work pass types:\n    - Employment Pass (EP): Minimum salary SGD 5,000/month\n    - S Pass: Minimum salary SGD 3,150/month\n    - Work Permit: No fixed minimum, sector-specific\n  - 5 Q&A items in FAQ section covering common questions:\n    - Diploma vs EP eligibility\n    - Salary negotiation strategy\n    - Family dependencies policies\n    - Actual processing timelines\n    - Job changing mid-pass restrictions\n  - Additional sections on red flags, individual pass details, and next steps\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Blog posts are stored as static TypeScript objects in `rawPosts` array\n- Reading time is auto-computed from word count (200 wpm baseline)\n- HTML content is embedded as template literals in TypeScript strings\n- Blog metadata includes slug, title, excerpt, date, category, author, readTime, tags, featuredImage\n\n### Gotchas\n- Content HTML must stay under 8000 characters for performance\n- All blog post fields are required; missing fields cause TypeScript compilation errors\n- Category must match existing categories or create new ones (checked against unique set)\n\n### Test results\n- Content size: 6,066 characters (well under 8,000 limit)\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (67 static pages including new blog post)\n- Blog post accessible via /blog/singapore-work-pass-guide-malaysians slug\n- All acceptance criteria met:\n  - ✓ Slug and category correct\n  - ✓ Comparison table with all 3 pass types and salary minimums\n  - ✓ FAQ section with 5 Q&As (exceeds 3 required)\n  - ✓ ReadTime '12 min read', date '2026-04-14'\n  - ✓ npm run build passes\n\n## Iteration US-196 - Fri, Apr 14, 2026\n\nCompleted: Integrate channel manager for live OTA availability synchronisation (ID: US-196)\n\n### What was implemented\n- **Beds24 client** (`site/src/lib/beds24-client.ts`):\n  - Implemented `parseAvailabilityResponse()` to parse real Beds24 API responses\n  - Returns proper `ChannelManagerAvailability` objects with room inventory\n  - Graceful fallback to placeholder data when channel manager is not configured\n- **ARI endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/ari/route.ts`):\n  - Fetches live availability from Beds24 API\n  - Maps to standard ARI format for connectivity partners (Google Free Booking Links)\n  - Returns JSON with property info, rooms, rates, and booking URL\n- **Booking sync endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/bookings/sync/route.ts`):\n  - Accepts POST requests with booking details (checkin, checkout, guest info, room type)\n  - Syncs bookings to Beds24 to close availability across OTAs\n  - Includes availability check via GET with query parameters\n- **Webhook endpoint** (`site/src/app/api/webhooks/beds24/route.ts`):\n  - Processes Beds24 webhook events for availability updates, booking creation/cancellation\n  - Validates webhook signature using HMAC-SHA256\n  - Logs event details (extensible for database updates)\n- **Configuration & types**:\n  - Channel manager config with environment variables\n  - Type definitions for availability, bookings, webhooks\n  - Updated .env.local.example with all required secrets\n\n### Patterns discovered\n- Next.js App Router uses async GET/POST functions\n- Beds24 API response structure uses `roomtypes` key with room availability objects\n- Response parsing should handle multiple field names (id/roomId, name/roomName) for flexibility\n- Placeholder data allows testing without configured channel manager\n\n### Gotchas\n- Beds24 API field names vary (id vs roomId, total vs totalInventory)\n- Parser must be defensive and return empty array on malformed responses\n- Webhook validation using HMAC requires exact payload matching (must use `request.text()` not `request.json()`)\n\n### Test results\n- TypeScript compilation: ✓ passes (npx tsc --noEmit)\n- Next.js build: ✓ succeeds (all 65 static pages generated)\n- ARI endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n- Webhook endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n- Booking sync endpoint: ✓ shows as dynamic route (ƒ)\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-196 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is for a hotel channel manager integration, which seems mismatched with the `lhdn_payroll_integration` app context. The following brief assumes the target is a hospitality-focused Frappe app (e.g., `pelangi_hospitality`).\n\n**Project:** US-196: Channel Manager Integration\n**Target App:** `pelangi_hospitality` (Assumed)\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/channel_manager.py` to encapsulate all communication with the third-party channel manager API (e.g., Beds24).\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/webhooks.py` to create a dedicated, whitelisted endpoint to receive incoming ARI updates and bookings from the channel manager.\n*   **Existing File:** `pelangi_hospitality/doctype/booking/booking.py` (or similar for reservations) to add logic that pushes new bookings to the channel manager.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocTypes:**\n    *   A new singleton `Channel Manager Settings` DocType is needed to store API keys, endpoint URLs, and credentials.\n    *   The `Booking` DocType will require modification to trigger syncs and store the channel manager's external booking ID.\n    *   The `Room` DocType needs a field to map its ID to the corresponding ID in the channel manager.\n*   **Hooks (`hooks.py`):**\n    *   `on_submit` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and reduce availability.\n    *   `on_cancel` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and increase availability.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Implement the API client with methods for fetching and updating availability.\n2.  Use the `Booking` DocType's server-side `after_insert` and `on_trash` events to trigger API calls.\n3.  Create a whitelisted API endpoint for the website frontend to call, which in turn fetches live availability from the channel manager API.\n4.  Implement the webhook endpoint to create/update local `Booking` documents from incoming OTA bookings.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Race Conditions:** Implement a locking mechanism or re-verify availability with the channel manager API just before creating a booking to prevent overbookings.\n*   **API Failure:** Use Frappe's background jobs (RQ) with a retry mechanism for all outgoing API calls to handle transient network issues or API downtime.\n*   **Webhook Security:** Validate incoming webhooks using a shared secret to prevent unauthorized requests.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-196 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis user story is for a hotel channel manager integration, which seems mismatched with the `lhdn_payroll_integration` app context. The following brief assumes the target is a hospitality-focused Frappe app (e.g., `pelangi_hospitality`).\n\n**Project:** US-196: Channel Manager Integration\n**Target App:** `pelangi_hospitality` (Assumed)\n\n**1. Python Files to Modify:**\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/channel_manager.py` to encapsulate all communication with the third-party channel manager API (e.g., Beds24).\n*   **New File:** `pelangi_hospitality/api/webhooks.py` to create a dedicated, whitelisted endpoint to receive incoming ARI updates and bookings from the channel manager.\n*   **Existing File:** `pelangi_hospitality/doctype/booking/booking.py` (or similar for reservations) to add logic that pushes new bookings to the channel manager.\n\n**2. Frappe DocTypes & Hooks:**\n*   **DocTypes:**\n    *   A new singleton `Channel Manager Settings` DocType is needed to store API keys, endpoint URLs, and credentials.\n    *   The `Booking` DocType will require modification to trigger syncs and store the channel manager's external booking ID.\n    *   The `Room` DocType needs a field to map its ID to the corresponding ID in the channel manager.\n*   **Hooks (`hooks.py`):**\n    *   `on_submit` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and reduce availability.\n    *   `on_cancel` on the `Booking` DocType to call `channel_manager.py` and increase availability.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Implement the API client with methods for fetching and updating availability.\n2.  Use the `Booking` DocType's server-side `after_insert` and `on_trash` events to trigger API calls.\n3.  Create a whitelisted API endpoint for the website frontend to call, which in turn fetches live availability from the channel manager API.\n4.  Implement the webhook endpoint to create/update local `Booking` documents from incoming OTA bookings.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Gotchas:**\n*   **Race Conditions:** Implement a locking mechanism or re-verify availability with the channel manager API just before creating a booking to prevent overbookings.\n*   **API Failure:** Use Frappe's background jobs (RQ) with a retry mechanism for all outgoing API calls to handle transient network issues or API downtime.\n*   **Webhook Security:** Validate incoming webhooks using a shared secret to prevent unauthorized requests.\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  3:40:40 AM\nCompleted: Integrate channel manager for live OTA availability synchronisation (ID: US-196) in 10m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-242 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nThis technical brief is for User Story US-242.\n\n**Correction:** The user story is for a **Next.js/TypeScript** website, not a Frappe/Python app as stated in the prompt. The instructions below are for the Next.js implementation based on the story's `filesTouch` and `acceptanceCriteria`.\n\n**1. Files to Modify:**\n*   `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`: This is the primary file. A new post object must be added to the `blogPosts` array.\n*   `site/src/data/blog/authors.ts`: (Likely file) Check this file to ensure the `authors.jay` object exists and is correctly imported/referenced in `posts.ts`.\n\n**2. DocTypes/Hooks & Concepts:**\n*   **Framework:** Next.js with TypeScript.\n*   **Data Structure:** The blog content is managed as a static array of objects within a `.ts` file, not a database or Frappe DocType.\n*   **Content:** The post body will be a large HTML string embedded within the new post object.\n\n**3. Implementation Approach:**\n1.  Define a new object for the blog post in `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`.\n2.  Populate metadata fields: `slug`, `category`, `date`, `author`, `readTime` as per the acceptance criteria.\n3.  Write the blog content as an HTML string, ensuring it covers all required topics (job portals, work passes, etc.).\n4.  Add internal links and use numbered lists as specified.\n5.  Insert the new object into the `blogPosts` array.\n6.  From the `site/` directory, run `npm run build` to verify the changes.\n\n**4. Gotchas & Edge Cases:**\n*   **Content Length:** The `technicalNotes` specify a hard limit of 8000 characters for the HTML content string, which may be challenging with a 1000+ word requirement. Brevity is key.\n*   **Build Verification:** The `npm run build` step is non-negotiable and must pass. Any TypeScript or linting error will fail the task.\n*   **Static Data:** Ensure correct TypeScript syntax for the new object and its properties, including correctly referencing the imported author object.\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-243 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\n**Subject: Technical Brief for US-243: Singapore Work Pass Guide Blog Post**\n\n**Note:** The user story points to a TypeScript/Next.js project (`site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`, `npm run build`), not a Frappe/Python application. This brief is tailored to the specified files.\n\n**1. Primary File for Modification:**\n*   `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`: A new blog post object needs to be created and added to the main exported array in this file.\n\n**2. Key Data Structures / Types:**\n*   The new object must conform to the existing `Post` data structure (or a similar TypeScript interface) used within `posts.ts`.\n*   This involves fields like `slug`, `title`, `content`, `category`, `readTime`, and `date`.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Define a new constant containing the blog post's content as an HTML string.\n*   This HTML string must feature a `<table>` to compare EP, S Pass, and Work Permit, incorporating the salary minimums from the story description.\n*   The HTML should also include a dedicated section for an FAQ with at least 3 Q&As.\n*   Construct the new post object using the metadata from the acceptance criteria (slug, category, readTime, date) and reference the HTML content string.\n*   Add this new object to the posts collection.\n\n**4. Edge Cases & Verification:**\n*   **Constraint:** The HTML content string must be kept under 8000 characters.\n*   **Syntax:** Ensure proper escaping for quotes and special characters within the TypeScript template literal for the HTML.\n*   **Validation:** After adding the post, run `npm run build` from the `site/` directory to ensure no build errors are introduced.\n\n## Iteration 1 - Tue, Apr 14, 2026  4:05:43 AM\nCompleted: Blog post: Singapore Work Pass Guide for Malaysians (EP vs S Pass vs WP) (ID: US-243) in 6m\n\n\n## Pre-analysis for US-244 (Gemini 2.5 Pro)\nBased on the user story JSON for **US-244**, there is a mismatch with the request. This story pertains to adding a blog post to a **TypeScript/Next.js website**, not a Frappe/Python application. The analysis below is for the story as provided.\n\n---\n\n### Technical Brief: US-244\n\n**1. Files to Modify:**\n*   `site/src/data/blog/posts.ts`: The story explicitly identifies this file. It likely contains an array of objects, where each object represents a blog post. A new object for this post needs to be added to this array.\n\n**2. Involved Frameworks/Data Structures:**\n*   The project is a Next.js website using TypeScript. No Frappe DocTypes or Python hooks are relevant.\n*   The core task is to create a new JavaScript/TypeScript object that conforms to the data structure used for existing posts in `posts.ts`.\n\n**3. Recommended Implementation Approach:**\n*   Create a new constant object for the blog post.\n*   Populate its properties:\n    *   `slug`: 'jb-singapore-commuter-guide-2026'\n    *   `category`: 'Career & Work'\n    *   `d